//! Buses: the signals of a serial interface, joined as one, and the pull-up resistors that
//! hold their lines.
//!
//! Who drives a signal is not said here. It is said by the pin kind in the pin table of the
//! part that has the bus (ADR 0016): the master's `mosi` is an `output` and the slave's is an
//! `input`, and the checker's rules do the rest. Two outputs on one net are refused, and an
//! `input` that is connected to nothing is refused, so a bus joined the wrong way round is found
//! without a rule of its own. Names are those of the device that has the bus. Levels are the
//! supply of the part (see `std::logic::Logic`).

import std::parts::passive::Resistor
import std::sources::Rail

/// A UART: a transmit line `tx`, a receive line `rx`, and the flow-control lines `rts` and
/// `cts`. The names are the device's own, so the lines of two devices cross: the board joins
/// one device's `tx` to the other's `rx` and the reverse, a pin at a time, because `~` between
/// two UARTs would join `tx` to `tx`. That is two outputs on one net, and the checker says so.
/// A device without flow control gives no pin to `rts` and `cts` and leaves them alone.
interface Uart {
    .tx: Pin
    .rx: Pin
    .rts: Pin
    .cts: Pin
}

/// An SPI bus: the clock `sclk`, the data out of the master `mosi`, the data out of the slave
/// `miso`, and the select `cs`. Both ends call the lines the same, so the master and the
/// slave join with `~`: the master's `mosi` is an output and the slave's an input, and the
/// pin kinds catch a master joined to a master.
interface Spi {
    .sclk: Pin
    .mosi: Pin
    .miso: Pin
    .cs: Pin
}

/// An I2C bus: the data line `sda` and the clock `scl`. Both are open-drain: they are pulled
/// to the supply by a resistor on the bus, one on each line, and no device drives them high.
/// `std::bus::I2cPullups` is that resistor pair. The checker refuses a line that has no
/// resistor to a rail's positive pin (ADR 0044).
interface I2c {
    .sda: Pin
    .scl: Pin
}

/// A serial-wire debug port: the data line `swdio`, the clock `swclk`, the trace output `swo`
/// and the reset `reset`. The target's `swo` is an output, its `swclk` an input and its `swdio`
/// a bidirectional pin; a probe's connector has passive pins.
interface Swd {
    .swdio: Pin
    .swclk: Pin
    .swo: Pin
    .reset: Pin
}

/// A USB 2.0 port: the data pair `dp` and `dm` and the bus power `vbus`, whose `pos` is VBUS
/// and whose `neg` is the ground. The host and the device join with `~`: D+ to D+ and D- to
/// D-. `vbus.current` is negative at the host, which supplies, and positive at a device that
/// draws.
interface Usb2 {
    .dp: Pin
    .dm: Pin
    .vbus: Rail
}

/// A pull-up resistor: one resistor from the positive pin of `supply` to `line`, which holds
/// the line high while nothing drives it. The board states the resistance, as a value with its
/// tolerance (`.pullup.resistance &= 10kohm +/- 5%`), and the solver picks a resistor that stays
/// inside it; a board that does not gets an unconstrained pick finding for the resistor, with
/// the member named (ADR 0065). Use it on a reset, an enable, an open-drain output or any one
/// line; `I2cPullups` is the same circuit twice, for the two lines of an I2C bus.
///
/// The module states what it draws from `supply`, so the net it is on has every current it
/// needs (ADR 0026): a plain `Resistor` between a rail and a line leaves the current of the
/// resistor unknown, the rail's net has two unknowns, and every check on that net waits. The
/// line is held low, at worst, for the share `line_low` of the time, 0 to 1 (a board that knows
/// its line is low only part of the time narrows it: `.pullup.line_low &= 0..0.1`). While it
/// is low the resistor carries the supply voltage over its resistance (the lowest the line
/// goes is 0 V; the line gives no low level of a driver, so this is the worst case, and a
/// driver's real level draws a little less), and while the line is released it carries nothing.
/// So the resistor's current, and with it the draw on `supply`, is 0 up to
/// `supply.voltage / resistance`. A board that states a current on `supply` itself states a
/// budget (ADR 0038): the draw must be able to fit in it. Two `PullUp`s on one rail add.
///
/// That is the steady draw, with two assumptions that the module offers and a board may
/// replace (ADR 0070):
///
/// - `overdrive`, how far above `supply` a driver holds the line, at most. It is 0 V: the line
///   is never driven above the supply. A line a 5 V driver pulls above a 3.3 V supply pushes
///   current back into the rail, a negative draw of up to `overdrive / resistance`, which a
///   board that has such a driver states (`.pull.overdrive &= 0V..1.7V`); the draw then
///   reaches below 0 and a budget of draws only fails.
/// - `supply_top`, the voltage the draw is worked out at. It is `supply.voltage`, the steady
///   range, which is what a budget and a power rating read. A board that wants the draw at
///   the transient its rail can reach states it (`.pull.supply_top &= .rail.peak`).
module PullUp {
    .supply: Rail
    .line: Pin
    .resistance: Resistance
    .resistor: Resistor
    .resistance == .resistor.resistance
    .supply.pos ~> .resistor ~> .line

    .line_low: Number
    .line_low &= 0..1
    .overdrive: Voltage
    .overdrive ?= 0V
    .supply_top: Voltage
    .supply_top ?= .supply.voltage
    .resistor.current == (.line_low * .supply_top - .overdrive) / .resistor.resistance
}

/// The two pull-up resistors of an I2C bus, one on `sda` and one on `scl`, to the positive
/// pin of `supply`. The board states the resistance, as a value with its tolerance
/// (`.pullups.resistance &= 4.7kohm +/- 5%`), and the solver picks resistors that stay inside
/// it. There is one pair for the whole bus, wherever it sits; two pairs on one bus are in
/// parallel and the board's concern.
///
/// The module states what it draws from `supply`, so the net it is on has every current it
/// needs (ADR 0026): a pull-up carries nothing while its line is released or its driver is off,
/// and at most the supply voltage over its resistance while the line is held low (the lowest the
/// line goes is 0 V; the bus interface gives no low level of a driver, so this is the worst case,
/// and a driver's real level draws a little less). `sda_low` and `scl_low` are the share of that
/// worst case, 0 to 1 each, and the two lines can be low at once. `supply.current` is the sum of
/// the two, worked out by the solver from the resistors the board's `.resistance` picks. A board
/// that states a current on `supply` itself states a budget (ADR 0038): the sum must be able to
/// fit in it. It is the steady draw, with the two assumptions of `PullUp`, offered the same
/// way: `overdrive` (0 V: no driver holds a line above `supply`; a negative draw of up to
/// `overdrive / resistance` otherwise) and `supply_top` (`supply.voltage`; the transient the
/// board states, for the draw at it). Both apply to both lines.
module I2cPullups {
    .bus: I2c
    .supply: Rail
    .resistance: Resistance
    .sda_pullup: Resistor
    .scl_pullup: Resistor
    .resistance == .sda_pullup.resistance
    .resistance == .scl_pullup.resistance
    .supply.pos ~> .sda_pullup ~> .bus.sda
    .supply.pos ~> .scl_pullup ~> .bus.scl

    .sda_low: Number
    .scl_low: Number
    .sda_low &= 0..1
    .scl_low &= 0..1
    .overdrive: Voltage
    .overdrive ?= 0V
    .supply_top: Voltage
    .supply_top ?= .supply.voltage
    .sda_pullup.current == (.sda_low * .supply_top - .overdrive) / .sda_pullup.resistance
    .scl_pullup.current == (.scl_low * .supply_top - .overdrive) / .scl_pullup.resistance
}
